PLACES=[
N('scott-ice-house-nutten-hook','R. and W. Scott Ice Company ice house ruins, Nutten Hook','Stuyvesant','hud','industry','ruins',42.35756,-73.78862,'exact','public','walk','','1885–1934',
  'Robert and William Scott built a huge ice house here in 1885, 200 by 300 feet and 40 feet high, holding about 53,000 tons of Hudson River ice cut each winter and shipped to New York City in summer. At its height the company employed about 100 men. Artificial ice and refrigerators ended the trade; the building was sold to the Knaust brothers to grow mushrooms and burned in 1934. Scenic Hudson calls it the largest of some 130 ice businesses in the valley.',
  'The ice house foundations, with walls two feet thick, the outer walls and chimney stack of the powerhouse of about 1885, and what is left of the dock and bulkhead, reached by a short loop trail over a rocky knoll in the river. Interpretive panels along the trail tell the story of the ice harvest.',
  'The biggest ice house on the Hudson, burned in 1934, its powerhouse stack still standing on a rock in the river.',
  'Nutten Hook Unique Area, part of the Stockport Flats site of the Hudson River National Estuarine Research Reserve, run by NYS DEC: open year-round, no fee, no reservation. Park at the end of Ice House Road, off NY 9J, where the trail begins, or at the Ferry Road lot, from which the River View Trail runs over the knoll to the ruins. Both have hand launches for canoes and kayaks.',
  [W('R. and W. Scott Ice Company Powerhouse and Ice House Site'),DEC,L('Scenic Hudson: Nutten Hook','https://www.scenichudson.org/explore-the-valley/outdoor-adventures/adventure/nutten-hook/'),HM(244953,"Natural Ice's Rise and Fall"),HM(244941,'Stockport Flats: The Nutten Hook Unique Area'),HM(132615,'Newton Hook')],
  th=[42.35832,-73.78739],thn='Ice House Road trailhead lot',brick='?',hours='Open year-round'),
N('stuyvesant-station','Stuyvesant station (Stuyvesant Landing depot)','Stuyvesant','hud','rail','standing',42.38889,-73.78361,'site','view','drive','stations','1880–1958',
  'The first station at Stuyvesant Landing burned, and this one was built in the second half of 1880 by Mull & Fromer, masons of Catskill. It served the New York Central\'s Water Level Route until 1958; one Hudson–Albany commuter round trip still stopped until the mid-1960s. The trains still run past it on the Hudson line.',
  'A five-bay brick depot, 50 by 20 feet, with a hipped roof, a molded and ornamented cornice and a curved canopy, by the tracks at Stuyvesant Landing. Wikipedia says a local farmers market is held at the station; its days were not checked. Interior not checked. A state marker for Stuyvesant Landing stands at NY 9J and Kinderhook Street.',
  'An 1880 brick depot on the Water Level Route, with trains still passing.',
  'Owner not checked. See it from the street; stay off the railroad, which is active. The nearest mapped parking is a town lot off Summerset Road, about 500 m northeast.',
  [W('Stuyvesant station'),HM(132610,'Stuyvesant Landing')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('linlithgo-reformed-church-livingston','Linlithgo Reformed Church of Livingston','Livingston','hud','worship','standing',42.14263,-73.77657,'exact','view','drive','','1854',
  'The Dutch Reformed congregation of the hamlet of Livingston, which was first called Johnstown after John Livingston, built this church in 1854. The 1935 state marker for Johnstown names the Linlithgo Reformed Church as one of the hamlet\'s old landmarks, with a Revolutionary stage inn and the post office of 1805.',
  'A two-story brick church in the Italianate style with a medium-pitched gable roof, a deep bracketed cornice and a tower set half into the middle of the front. The cemetery beside it holds several hundred burials from about 1814 on. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1854 Italianate brick church at the heart of the old hamlet of Johnstown.',
  'At 447 Church Road in the hamlet of Livingston, a short way from the town hall on Old Post Road (County Route 19). Whether the congregation still holds services was not checked: see it from the road and the cemetery in daylight.',
  [W('Linlithgo Reformed Church of Livingston'),HM(136497,'Johnstown')],
  brick='?'),
N('livingston-town-hall','Livingston Town Hall and the Johnstown markers','Livingston','hud','civic','standing',42.14188,-73.77827,'exact','view','drive','','1895',
  'The Town of Livingston was formed in 1788. Its town hall was approved at a meeting in Walter Haynor\'s house in August 1895 and built by Walter Haynor Jr. for $1,648, on land conveyed by Solon and Ellen Smith that April. The hamlet around it was first named Johnstown after John Livingston, a son of the third lord of Livingston Manor; its post office opened in 1805.',
  'The 1895 section of the town hall on Old Post Road (County Route 19), a white clapboard building with a cupola in a photograph on Wikimedia Commons, with a cluster of markers in the small plaza to its west: the 1935 state marker for Johnstown, a Town of Livingston 1788 marker, a marker for Alida Livingston, who managed her husband\'s manor, and one on the town hall itself. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1895 country town hall with the manor\'s story told on markers outside.',
  'Town of Livingston offices. The markers stand in the plaza beside Old Post Road and can be read any time; office hours were not checked.',
  [HM(136498,'Livingston Town Hall'),HM(136497,'Johnstown'),HM(136499,'Alida Livingston'),HM(136496,'Town of Livingston - 1788')],
  brick='no'),
N('glenco-mills-methodist-chapel','Glenco Mills Methodist Chapel','Glenco Mills, Livingston','hud','worship','standing',42.1504,-73.7418,'site','view','drive','','1869',
  'Glenco Mills was a mill hamlet on Claverack Creek, called Sober until after 1830; in 1878 it had a flour mill, a saw mill, shops, a hotel and about eighty people. The chapel was built in 1869 for the mill workers and especially their children, paid for by Isaac Shaurman, who was born in Livingston, made his career in New York City and retired here. It was listed on the National Register in 2019.',
  'A small wood-frame Gothic Revival chapel with clapboard walls, a bell tower, steep roofs, diamond-pane sash and peaked door and window crowns, on the west side of Old Route 82 just south of Weaver Road, with a 1940 parish hall beside it. The Register form describes the inside as remarkably intact: beaded board walls and ceiling, the original slip pews, a raised dais, a 19th-century Estey harmonium and the old kerosene fixtures, now electrified. Whether and when it opens was not checked.',
  'A mill hamlet\'s 1869 chapel, its pews, harmonium and kerosene lamps still in place.',
  'Owner and services not checked. See it from Old Route 82; there is no parking lot, so stop only where the shoulder is safe.',
  [L('National Register form: Glenco Mills Methodist Chapel (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/19a3c470-bab3-478d-acfb-04c3d03f3808'),L('History of Columbia County (1878): Glenco Mills','https://usgennet.org/usa/ny/county/columbia/livstn/glenco_mills.htm')],
  brick='no'),
N('gallatin-reformed-church','Gallatin Reformed Church and cemetery (Gallatin Community Church)','Gallatinville, Gallatin','hud','worship','standing',42.01958,-73.7029,'exact','view','drive','cemeteries','1748; church 1823–1824',
  'This was the Greenbush Dutch Reformed Church, also called the Vedder Church; its first baptism was recorded in 1748 and the first grave marked in 1770. Its pastor before and during the Revolution, John H. Livingston, was a strong supporter of independence, and militia captain Hugh Rea lies in the cemetery with more than a dozen of his men. The first building was condemned and the present white meeting house went up in 1823–1824, and was altered in 1872 to take an organ. Its pastor calls it the mother church of fourteen congregations in the area. Church, cemetery and the former parsonage were listed on the National Register in 2021.',
  'A white clapboard meeting house of the Wren–Gibbs type, with a tall stepped tower in a 2024 photograph on Wikimedia Commons, at the foot of a hillside cemetery with a wide range of old gravestones, and the former parsonage, at 234 County Route 7. In 2021 a small all-volunteer congregation still worshipped here. Interior not checked.',
  'The 1748 mother church of the manor\'s Reformed congregations, with Revolutionary militiamen in its hillside graveyard.',
  'Active church (The Millerton News, 2021); service times not checked. The cemetery can be seen from County Route 7; no parking lot was found.',
  [L('The Millerton News: Gallatin Community Church to join National Register (2021)','https://millertonnews.com/millerton-news-regional/gallatin-community-church-join-national-historic-register'),L('History Pointer: Register listing','https://historypointer.com/nris/resource/100006759')],
  brick='no'),
N('stone-jug-clermont','The Stone Jug (Konradt Lasher house)','Clermont','hud','landmark','standing',42.10361,-73.89611,'site','view','drive','','1752',
  'Konradt Lasher, a Palatine German who came as one of the refugees settled on Livingston Manor in 1710 to make naval stores, farmed here as a tenant of the Livingstons and by 1752 had built this house near his brothers George and Johannis. He chose stone where his neighbours favoured brick. His descendants bought the land in the mid-19th century and built the farmhouse across the road. The 1955 state marker calls it one of the oldest houses in the Town of Clermont.',
  'A story-and-a-half stone house with a gabled roof, its west gable end and chimney in brick, at the corner of NY 9G and Jug Road. The lintel over the east door is carved with 1752 and "K.B.L & J." and the original Dutch doors survive. The farm also has a later farmhouse, an L-shaped frame barn south of Jug Road and the foundations of two other stone houses. Interior not checked.',
  'A Palatine farmer\'s stone house of 1752 with the date cut over the door.',
  'Private house. See it from NY 9G or Jug Road; do not go onto the property. No parking lot.',
  [W('Stone Jug'),HM(77137,'The Stone Jug')],
  brick='?'),
N('west-taghkanic-diner','West Taghkanic Diner','Taghkanic','hud','landmark','standing',42.11957,-73.71736,'site','open','drive','now','1953',
  'When the Taconic State Parkway ended at NY 82, Burt Coons opened diners at its end, the first of four Chief Taghkanic diners. This one is a large Mountain View factory-built diner, serial number 399, from 1953 according to a newspaper piece quoted on a car-history blog, and still stands where it was put. A chef, Kristopher Schram, bought and reopened it.',
  'A stainless-steel Mountain View diner never bricked over or given a mansard roof, in original condition inside and out according to a roadside-history site, which also describes an Indian-head neon sign. A 2023 newspaper headline said the diner would remove the Native American symbol; a June 2022 photograph on Wikimedia Commons still shows the sign; whether it is up now was not checked.',
  'A 1953 parkway diner still serving, one of the last of the Chief Taghkanic chain.',
  'A working diner on NY 82 just west of the Taconic State Parkway, with its own lot. Opening days and hours were not checked.',
  [L('Roadside Fans: Taconic Parkway, New York\'s diner drive','https://www.roadsidefans.com/?p=639'),L('Just a Car Guy: Burt Coons diners on the Taconic','https://justacarguy.blogspot.com/2022/05/burt-coons-diners-on-taghkanic-parkway.html')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('copake-railroad-depot','Copake Railroad Depot','Copake','hud','rail','standing',42.0952,-73.5443,'area','view','drive','stations','1876; freight house 1913',
  'Built in 1876 for the Rhinebeck & Connecticut Railroad, this small passenger station has a freight house of 1913 joined to it. The state calls it one of only three of the company\'s depots that survive and the only one never moved or much altered. It was nominated to the National Register in 2023.',
  'A one-story wood-frame depot with its original board siding, a gabled roof with deep overhanging eaves and Gothic touches, joined to a one-story freight house. A 2024 photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows it weathered, with some windows boarded. Interior not checked.',
  'The one Rhinebeck & Connecticut depot still where it was built and as it was built.',
  'Owner not checked. See it from the road only. The pin is the list position, not checked against the building.',
  [L('Governor\'s office: 2023 State and National Register nominations','https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-announces-37-nominations-state-and-national-registers-historic-places')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('mellenville-station','Mellenville station (Mellenville Grange)','Mellenville, Claverack','hud','rail','standing',42.25306,-73.66806,'area','view','drive','stations','about 1900',
  'The Boston and Albany Railroad built this depot on its Hudson branch around 1900. It stopped being a station in the 1930s, when the Grange bought it, and it is known as the Mellenville Grange. It was listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'A one-story brick depot with a slate hipped roof whose wide overhang rests on heavy scroll-sawn wooden brackets, and the station privy beside it. Interior not checked.',
  'A bracketed 1900 depot that has been a Grange hall for ninety years.',
  'Grange property as far as the sources say; whether it is still in use was not checked. See it from the road.',
  [W('Mellenville station')],
  brick='?'),
N('craryville-station','Craryville station','Craryville, Copake','hud','rail','standing',42.17473,-73.58022,'site','view','drive','stations','1852–1972',
  'The New York and Harlem Railroad built its line through Craryville between 1849 and 1852 on the way to Chatham, and the station served a hamlet with busy industry. Under the New York Central it handled passengers and freight, with a high freight dock. Passenger trains ended on March 20, 1972, and freight in 1976, when the tracks north of Wassaic were taken up.',
  'The former station beside NY 23 in Craryville. The Harlem Valley Rail Trail Association plans to carry the trail along the old line in front of it. Its date of building and present use were not checked; interior not checked.',
  'A Harlem Division depot left by the road when the trains stopped in 1972.',
  'Owner not checked. See it from NY 23 only.',
  [W('Craryville station')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('snyderville-schoolhouse','Snyderville Schoolhouse','Snyderville, Livingston','hud','civic','standing',42.08121,-73.75244,'exact','view','drive','','about 1860–1942',
  'A one-room district school built about 1860 in the hamlet of Snyderville, which taught children until 1942. It was listed on the National Register in 2002.',
  'A small one-story wood-frame schoolhouse with clapboard siding, a gable roof and a little square bell tower on top. Interior not checked.',
  'A one-room school with its bell tower, in use until 1942.',
  'Owner not checked. See it from the road.',
  [W('Snyderville Schoolhouse')],
  brick='no'),
N('livingston-manor-house-site','Livingston Manor house site (first manor house, 1699)','Linlithgo','hud','landmark','marker',42.17737,-73.85384,'area','public','drive','','1699',
  'In 1686 Governor Dongan confirmed to Robert Livingston a manor of 160,000 acres along the Hudson, and in 1715 a new patent gave it a seat in the colonial assembly. Robert and his wife Alida built their manor house in 1699 near where the Roeliff Jansen Kill meets the Hudson. A 1986 Town of Livingston marker for the manor\'s 300th year marks the spot.',
  'A roadside marker at NY 9G and Station Road. The house itself is gone and no remains are described in the sources read. The Livingston Memorial Church, built over the family crypt, is about 900 m to the southeast.',
  'Where the first lord of Livingston Manor built his house in 1699.',
  'The marker stands on the roadside of NY 9G, a state highway, at Station Road: read it from the shoulder. The land toward the river was not checked for ownership; do not go onto it.',
  [HM(132580,'Manor House Site'),HM(144665,'Livingston Manor')],
  brick='no'),
]
